MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The trick is +coord_polar()
in your application you would want geom_line() + coord_polar()
the more usual wind rose uses geom_bar() + coord_polar()

Note that to do this, you need to reshape your dataset into long format: species, attribute name, value.  In your plot you are then mapping a factor (attribute name: sepal.length, sepal.width, etc.) into an order around the circle and connecting the dots between those categories, which would be perceived as areas by the audience, even though only the differences at the 4 data points are real.  The order of the categories will matter a great deal for whether different species appear different in your example.

In other words, it is easy to create this graph in ggplot2, but often (not always) it is an inappropriate and potentially misleading visualization of the data, especially for multiple variables that become factors mapped to the direction.

Tom 2

